<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Wilmot Blyden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1832">1832</a>–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Wilmot Blyden was a writer, educator and statesman born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">Danish West Indies</a> who emigrated to Liberia in 1850. He taught at and led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-liberia">Liberia College</a>, served as Liberian secretary of state and envoy, and in works such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the Negro Race (1887) argued for the dignity of African cultures, becoming a founding thinker of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-wilmot-blyden.jpg" alt="Edward Wilmot Blyden" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johnston, Harry Hamilton, Sir, 1858-1927; Royal Geographical Society (Great Britain) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E._W._Blyden_Johnston.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edward Wilmot Blyden was born on 3 August 1832 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-u-s-virgin-islands">Saint Thomas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">Danish West Indies</a>, to free black parents who claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo people</a> of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. Between 1842 and 1845 the family lived in Porto Bello, Venezuela, where he became fluent in Spanish. In 1845 he met the Reverend John P. Knox, a Dutch Reformed pastor, who became his mentor and encouraged his aptitude for oratory and literature.</p><p>In May 1850 Blyden went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to enrol at Rutgers Theological College and was refused admission because of his race, as he was at two other colleges. Knox then encouraged him to go to Liberia, the colony set up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>, and he sailed there later in 1850.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Monrovia Blyden studied at Alexander High School, became its principal in 1858 and was ordained a Presbyterian minister that year. He edited the Liberia Herald in 1855 and 1856, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> in the early 1870s he founded and edited the newspaper The Negro. In 1861 he became professor of Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-liberia">Liberia College</a> and later served as its president, from 1880 to 1884.</p><p>He was Liberia's Secretary of State from 1864 to 1865 under Daniel Bashiel Warner and Secretary of the Interior from 1880 to 1882 under Anthony W. Gardner, and he served as Liberian ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He lost the 1885 presidential election as the Republican candidate. From 1901 to 1906 he directed the education of Sierra Leonean Muslims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a>.</p><p>Blyden believed that Black Americans could end their suffering from racial discrimination by returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and helping to develop it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> who wanted full civil rights in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> criticised him for it. He called this redemptive role Ethiopianism, compared the diaspora's suffering to that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and supported the Zionist project.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Blyden is regarded by some as the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism</a> and was among the first to articulate an African Personality. His major work, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the Negro Race (1887), argued that Islam was more unifying and fulfilling for Africans than Christianity, which he thought had a demoralising effect, although he remained a Christian. At first many doubted that an African had written it, and in later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printings</a> he added his photograph as the frontispiece. His ideas influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey">Marcus Garvey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-padmore">George Padmore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a>. Blyden died on 7 February 1912 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> and was buried at Racecourse Cemetery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Wilmot Blyden” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its early statesmen and scholars, especially <b>Edward Wilmot Blyden</b>, who taught at Liberia College and wrote on African identity, helped shape Pan-Africanism, and Marcus Garvey’s movement looked to Liberia in the early 1920s as a possible homeland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Martin Delany in the United States called for a Black nation, and <b>Edward Wilmot Blyden</b>, born in the Danish West Indies, settled in Liberia, defended the worth of African societies and their history in Christianity, Islam and the Negro Race (1887) and spoke of a distinct “African personality”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-u-s-virgin-islands">Saint Thomas</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.35,-64.94&amp;year=1884" aria-label="Saint Thomas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2790 1483 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Edward Wilmot Blyden"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3240 1753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3240 1753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.35,-64.94&amp;year=1884">What was here in 1884? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
